Is the Photoelectric Effect topic in JEE Advanced or JEE Main syllabus?

 The photoelectric effect is part of both JEE Main and JEE Advanced syllabi under Modern Physics. JEE Main tests it more straightforwardly, while JEE Advanced combines it with electrostatics, dimensional analysis, or quantum numbers - as seen in problems from 2011, 2015, and 2016.

What is the formula for stopping potential in JEE Advanced problems?

Stopping potential V₀ is given by eV₀ = hν - φ, or equivalently eV₀ = (hc/λ) - φ. The slope of the V₀ vs frequency graph equals h/e, which is the same for all metals regardless of their work function. This is a frequently tested fact in JEE Advanced.

How many marks does Photoelectric Effect carry in JEE Advanced?

Photoelectric Effect typically contributes 4-12 marks per paper in JEE Advanced, appearing in single correct, multi-correct, integer, or paragraph-based formats. Over 2009-2023, it appeared in at least 10 of 15 papers, making it one of the highest-frequency topics in Modern Physics.

How do I calculate the de Broglie wavelength of a particle accelerated through a potential difference?

Use λ = h/√(2mqV), where m is the particle mass, q is its charge, and V is the accelerating potential. For an electron: q = e. For an alpha particle: q = 2e and m = 4m_p. Always substitute the correct charge - this is the most common error in JEE Advanced de Broglie problems.

Why does the slope of the stopping potential vs. frequency graph not depend on the metal?

The slope of the V₀ vs ν graph equals h/e - Planck's constant divided by electronic charge. Since both h and e are universal constants, the slope is the same for every metal. Only the x-intercept (threshold frequency) changes depending on the work function. JEE Advanced 2013 tested this directly.

What is the difference between work function and threshold frequency?

Work function φ is the minimum energy required to eject a photoelectron, measured in eV. Threshold frequency ν₀ is the minimum frequency of light that can cause emission, given by ν₀ = φ/h. Below this frequency, no photoelectrons are emitted regardless of light intensity - a key conceptual point tested in JEE Advanced. Leave a comment
