(A,C) Let $\mathrm{n}_{1}$ harmonic is corresponding to 50.$7\text{ cm}$ & $\mathrm{n}_{2}$ harmonic is corresponding 83.$9\text{ cm}$. since both one consecutive harmonics. $\therefore$ their difference $=\frac{\lambda}{2}$ $\therefore \frac{\lambda}{2}=(83.9-50.7) \mathrm{cm}$ $\frac{\lambda}{2}=33.2 \mathrm{cm}$ $\lambda=66.4 \mathrm{cm}$ $\therefore \frac{\lambda}{4}=16.6 \mathrm{cm}$ length corresponding to fundamental mode must be close to \frac{\lambda}{4} \& 50.$7\text{ cm}$ must be an odd multiple of this length 16.6 × 3 = 49.$8\text{ cm}$. therefore 50.7 is 3^{| \mathrm{rd}}harmonic If end correction is e, then \mathrm{e}+50.7=\frac{3 \lambda}{4} \mathrm{e}=49.8-50.7=-0.9 \mathrm{cm} \text { speed of sound, } \mathrm{v}=\mathrm{f} \lambda \therefore \mathrm{v}=500 \times 66.4 \mathrm{cm} / \mathrm{sec}=332.000 \mathrm{m} / \mathrm{s} For students who find these Waves on a String questions too easy, our Irodov questions for JEE includes several challenging Waves on a String problems that mirror the JEE Advanced difficulty level.Waves on a String - JEE Advanced PYQs (Physics) can be followed by complete-paper practice through the JEE Advanced PYQ archive. ×
